  "tour": {
    "title": "John’s New Creation Through Genesis Eyes",
    "intro": "These clips treat John’s “new creation” as both a reading strategy and a claim about Jesus: John opens with Genesis 1, follows its light, water, temple, and garden patterns, and brings them to a climax in resurrection. You’ll hear where the teachers make an explicit connection and where the tour invites you to notice an echo for yourself.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:why-are-there-4-gospels",
        "startSec": 1950,
        "endSec": 2130,
        "why": "Start here for the controlling clue. The speakers say John’s opening is “the beginning of the new creation story,” with Genesis 1’s creation light reappearing as “God’s word” shining in darkness. They also begin to connect John’s Word with Proverbs 8’s personified Wisdom, so you have both the obvious Genesis echo and the deeper background in view.",
        "episodeTitle": "Why Are There Four Accounts of the Gospel?",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/5a781ce0-f80e-4a08-a13c-ca2ffb3d0c7a/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=5a781ce0-f80e-4a08-a13c-ca2ffb3d0c7a",
        "durationSec": 4125,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "forty-minutes-ot:podcast:807d2da752ed662a0b6413e24f92a20b",
        "startSec": 1880,
        "endSec": 2200,
        "why": "This gives the tour its interpretive thesis. The teacher says John is “presenting salvation from the perspective of creation” and that “redemption is God recreating,” not a separate divine project. The clip then explains why the Word becoming flesh matters: in Christ, the speaker says, “we gain more in Christ than we lost in Adam,” because creation is restored in the incarnate Christ.",
        "episodeTitle": "40 Minutes in the Old Testament: Episode 1",
        "sourceId": "forty-minutes-ot",
        "source": "40 Minutes in the Old Testament",
        "audioUrl": "https://adbarker.com/stream/tfVE3XBxC9JCmb97VsZt7vpT/traffic.libsyn.com/secure/40minot/40_OT_EP_1.mp3?dest-id=245197",
        "durationSec": 2527,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "spoken-gospel:podcast:4613377c-3e5d-11f1-b7fb-27eb7613d571",
        "startSec": 2860,
        "endSec": 3040,
        "why": "Now move from cosmic language to place and presence. The hosts hear “the word became flesh” as the Word becoming the “new tabernacle,” then connect that to their claim that “Eden was a temple” and the tabernacle a kind of new Eden. This makes the Genesis allusion spatial and embodied: Jesus is presented as the meeting place where God’s life enters creation.",
        "episodeTitle": "John 1: In the Beginning Was the Word",
        "sourceId": "spoken-gospel",
        "source": "Spoken Gospel",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/SPG8007534722.mp3",
        "durationSec": 3919,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "spoken-gospel:podcast:db54663c-6a8e-11f1-8d65-4723e9decce5",
        "startSec": 2600,
        "endSec": 2780,
        "why": "This tests the thesis in one of John’s recurring water scenes. The hosts describe Jesus as living water who “turns them into new creation, new humanity, Garden of Eden, founts of water themselves,” and then identify the dangerous water in the walking-on-water story as “Genesis 1 water that needs to be conquered.” Listen for new creation as both healing a person and bringing order over primordial chaos.",
        "episodeTitle": "John Overview: Living Water",
        "sourceId": "spoken-gospel",
        "source": "Spoken Gospel",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/SPG6305125345.mp3",
        "durationSec": 4717,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:d2454984-com-96e88155-08af-3240-94ab-4e13ecb0d2e9",
        "startSec": 540,
        "endSec": 700,
        "why": "Here the Genesis garden echo appears at the resurrection itself. The commentator walks through Mary’s grief and recognition, including the detail that she is “thinking he was the gardener.” The clip gives you the concrete scene—an empty tomb, a woman, a garden-like setting, and renewed life—so you can place John’s resurrection narrative beside Genesis 2–3.",
        "episodeTitle": "John 20:1-31",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/z8yyjpv9fmtit6hd/john_201-3185o5y.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1726,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:lord-sabbath",
        "startSec": 3680,
        "endSec": 3860,
        "why": "Finish with John’s creation-shaped timing. The speakers emphasize that Jesus rises “on the eighth day,” the “first day of new creation” and the first day of a new week, explicitly invoking Genesis 1. They also say the new light is hope that “day seven really is coming this time,” which holds together new creation already begun in Jesus and its still-awaited completion.",
        "episodeTitle": "Lord of the Sabbath",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/a685d984-7b8c-452b-b01c-d87d2968354a/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=a685d984-7b8c-452b-b01c-d87d2968354a",
        "durationSec": 4208,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      }
    ],
    "closing": "Taken together, the tour’s answer is not that John is decorating his Gospel with Genesis language. The teachers describe Jesus as the Word through whom creation came, the embodied temple who restores humanity, and the risen one who opens the eighth-day week. The Genesis allusions make “new creation” mean creation repaired and carried toward its promised fullness—already begun in Jesus, still moving toward the final Sabbath.",
    "totalSeconds": 1200
  },
